# Appendix: memdb memory-layer architecture (current, 2026-08-31)

Reference for any agent session on this project. This describes the
cross-host memory system the sessions record into and read from. It is
maintenance-free from the rig agent's point of view: read it so you know
where session memory comes from and how to record into it.

## Design: log-first, projection-derived

- The SYSTEM OF RECORD is a per-host, append-only, hash-chained event
  journal: `~/.claude/memory/journal/journal/<host>/events.jsonl`. Every
  event carries `(host, seq, type, payload, ts, prev-hash)`; the chain
  hash makes tampering and truncation detectable (`verify` proves it).
- The SQLite DB (`$MEMDB_PATH`, default `~/.claude/memory/mem.db`) is a
  DISPOSABLE PROJECTION. `rebuild` reproduces it from journals alone;
  `verify` replays the journals and proves the live DB matches the
  canonical replay (`projection_match`) and every host chain is intact.
  Never edit journal files or the DB by hand.
- Host identity comes from `~/.claude/memory/host-id` (or `$MEMDB_HOST`);
  writes refuse to run without it. Current hosts: `workstation`,
  `laptop`, `pi`.
- Replication is git-based: one branch per host journal, hub on a
  self-hosted private remote. `sync/memdb_sync.sh` commits this host's
  journal branch, pushes, ingests the other hosts' journals, rebuilds
  and verifies. Records get a stable cross-host uid `<host>:<seq>`
  (episode children `<host>:<seq>/<n>`); local integer ids are
  reassigned by every rebuild and must never be used for addressing.

## Tool

`<memdb-repo>/memdb/mem.py` — Python 3 stdlib only. Subcommands:
`init`, `add`, `get`, `search`, `supersede`, `close`, `record-episode`,
`render-handoff`, `digest`, `export`, `consolidate`, `rebuild`,
`verify`, `doctor`, `redact-check`.

## Data model (schema v3)

One `records` table; memory type is the `kind` column:

- `episode` — one completed job (achievement / decisions / state /
  blockers / next steps as JSON payload; full raw text FTS-indexed at
  low rank weight).
- `decision`, `blocker`, `state` — auto-extracted children of an
  episode (`record-episode` parses the JSON doc); blockers named in a
  later episode's achievements auto-close.
- `goal`, `constraint`, `preference` — durable semantic records; the
  `core-setup` part holds session-start invariants (tool paths,
  hazards, standing preferences).
- `runbook` — NEW in v3: a proven-good command for a task.
  `subject` = task slug, `body` = the exact command that ran
  successfully, optional `rationale` = cwd/date/notes.

Write gating (`add` and episode children): same
`(project, kind, subject)` open record with the same normalized body →
re-confirm (bump `last_confirmed_at`); different body → insert new and
supersede all open matches; episodes are never superseded (close only).
Status lifecycle: `open` → `closed` or `superseded`. FTS5 index over
subject/body/rationale/fulltext with bm25 weights 5/1/1/0.5.

## Retrieval

- `digest --project <slug> [--part <part>]` — token-budgeted context
  (default 1500). Pinned, never trimmed: goals/constraints/preferences
  (project + `global`), and the runbook section "Proven commands (run
  these first, exactly)". Runbooks are project-wide, NOT part-scoped,
  so they surface in the session-start `--part core-setup` digest.
  Trimmable tail: open blockers, live decisions, current state, last 3
  episodes.
- `search --query ...` — summary lines (uid, subject, snippet); expand
  a hit with `get <uid>`. Never default to `--full`.

## Runbook usage (the "run exact command first" contract)

Record a command that ran successfully:

    mem add --kind runbook --project <slug> --part <part> \
      --subject "<task-slug>" --body "<exact command>" \
      [--rationale "<cwd/notes/date>"]

Re-recording the same command re-confirms it (floats up, newest
confirmation first); a changed command supersedes the old one. A later
session sees pinned runbooks in its digest and runs them FIRST, exactly
as recorded, before deriving anything new.

CROSS-HOST HAZARD: `runbook` events replay only on hosts running
schema-v3 memdb code (older CHECK constraints reject the kind). Update
the memdb code on every host BEFORE syncing/ingesting a journal that
contains runbook events, then `rebuild`.

## Privacy model

Journals and DB are the PRIVATE memory domain (user machines + the
self-hosted hub): capture is full-fidelity, never pre-redacted.
Redaction happens at the publish boundary at full strength: anything
leaving the private domain must pass `mem redact-check <file>` (exit 2
+ span report on hits). This appendix is itself redacted accordingly.
